{
  "AIF": {
    "nodes": [
      {
        "nodeID": 1,
        "type": "L",
        "text": "Bernard Suits: aside from bureaucratic practice, in anything but a game the gratuitous introduction of unnecessary obstacles to the achievement of an end is regarded as a decidedly irrational thing to do"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 2,
        "type": "L",
        "text": "Bernard Suits: in games it appears to be an absolutely essential thing to do"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 3,
        "type": "L",
        "text": "Bernard Suits: This fact about games has led some observers to conclude that there is something inherently absurd about games, or that games must involve a fundamental paradox"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 4,
        "type": "L",
        "text": "Observer: there is something inherently absurd about games"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 5,
        "type": "L",
        "text": "Observer: games must involve a fundamental paradox"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 6,
        "type": "L",
        "text": "Bernard Suits: This kind of view seems to me to be mistaken"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 7,
        "type": "L",
        "text": "Bernard Suits: The mistake consists in applying the same standard to games that is applied to means-end activities which are not games"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 8,
        "type": "L",
        "text": "Bernard Suits: If playing a game is regarded as not essentially different from going to the office or writing a cheque, then there is certainly something absurd or paradoxical or, more plausibly, simply something stupid about game playing"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 9,
        "type": "YA",
        "text": "Asserting"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 10,
        "type": "YA",
        "text": "Asserting"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 11,
        "type": "I",
        "text": "Introducing unnecessary obstacles to achieving an end in means-end activities that are not games is irrational",
        "pta": {
          "subject": "introducing unnecessary obstacles to achieving an end in means-end activities that are not games",
          "predicate": "is irrational",
          "kind": "V",
          "scope": "u",
          "note": "'aside from bureaucratic practice' is a joke and is dropped; 'is regarded as' is an illocutive marker. Worded like L7 so the undercut names the same standard."
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 12,
        "type": "YA",
        "text": "Asserting",
        "pta": {
          "transformations": [
            "same term"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 13,
        "type": "I",
        "text": "Games essentially involve introducing unnecessary obstacles to achieving an end",
        "pta": {
          "subject": "games",
          "predicate": "essentially involve introducing unnecessary obstacles to achieving an end",
          "kind": "F",
          "scope": "u",
          "note": "Recast with 'games' as subject so it shares a subject with the observers' conclusion. F: a claim about what games consist of."
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 14,
        "type": "YA",
        "text": "Asserting",
        "pta": {
          "transformations": [
            "anaphora",
            "explication"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 15,
        "type": "I",
        "text": "Games are inherently absurd",
        "pta": {
          "subject": "games",
          "predicate": "are inherently absurd",
          "kind": "V",
          "scope": "u"
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 16,
        "type": "YA",
        "text": "Asserting",
        "pta": {
          "transformations": [
            "same term"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 17,
        "type": "I",
        "text": "Games involve a fundamental paradox",
        "pta": {
          "subject": "games",
          "predicate": "involve a fundamental paradox",
          "kind": "V",
          "scope": "u"
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 18,
        "type": "YA",
        "text": "Asserting"
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 19,
        "type": "I",
        "text": "The view that games are inherently absurd is unacceptable",
        "pta": {
          "subjectNode": "15",
          "acceptability": "U",
          "kind": "V",
          "scope": "s",
          "note": "'This kind of view' covers both formulations; anchored to the first, which the second restates."
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 20,
        "type": "YA",
        "text": "Asserting",
        "pta": {
          "transformations": [
            "anaphora"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 21,
        "type": "I",
        "text": "The standard applied to means-end activities that are not games does not apply to games",
        "pta": {
          "subject": "the standard applied to means-end activities that are not games",
          "predicate": "does not apply to games",
          "kind": "V",
          "scope": "u"
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 22,
        "type": "YA",
        "text": "Asserting",
        "pta": {
          "transformations": [
            "explication"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "nodeID": 23,
        "type": "I",
        "text": "The view that games are inherently absurd implies that game playing is simply stupid",
        "pta": {
          "subjectNode": "15",
          "predicate": "implies that game playing is simply stupid",
          "kind": "V",
          "scope": "s",
          "perspective": "II",
          "note": "A reductio, not a concession: judged like going to the office, games come out simply stupid, which nobody accepts."
        }
      },
